# D10: no UI stall over 8 ms during a 200 Hz telemetry storm, measured on the frame.
#
# MP_STORM=200 replaces the link with an in-memory vehicle (mp_link::testing::Storm) that writes
# ATTITUDE, GLOBAL_POSITION_INT, VFR_HUD and HEARTBEAT 200 times a second each through the real
# link thread and snapshot bus, flying a circle so the HUD and the map change on every frame. The
# screen repaints at display rate. Each frame is timed from the top of render to the end of paint,
# less the harness's own facts and probe writes (crates/mp-gui/src/storm.rs). Frames from the
# first 2 s are not counted, and the facts appear once 100 frames have been counted, so reading
# them too early is a missing fact, not a pass.
#
# frame.p99 and frame.max are whole milliseconds rounded down, so `< 8` holds exactly when the
# time is under 8 ms; frame.p99_us, frame.max_us and frame.gap.*_us carry the microseconds for
# the record. storm.rate is the storm as the link counted it, in Hz per message.
#
# No map imagery (the harness's default), so the frame does not depend on what a tile cache
# happens to hold.
#
# The budget is the product's, so the number that counts is the release build's:
#   cargo build --release -p mp-gui
#   MP_GUI_BIN=target/release/planner tools/gui-test.sh tests/gui/storm.gui
# The debug build the other scripts use runs our own crates unoptimised; measured on 2026-09-24
# it gave p99 26 ms with 127 stalls in 480 frames at 200 Hz, and that is not the claim.

env MP_STORM 200
# Not against the debug build: its number is not the claim, so a suite skips this (exit 3).
setup case "${MP_GUI_BIN:-}" in *release*) ;; *) echo 'storm needs the release build: MP_GUI_BIN=target/release/planner' >&2; exit 3;; esac
screen fly
window 1600x1200
budget 12
# Recorded from the release run of 2026-09-26 (10.2 s from its window).


expect vehicle.connected true
expect vehicle.count 1
# Ten seconds of storm: a measurement window, not a wait for a fact.
settle 10
expect frame.count >= 100
# For the record, before the budget: the distribution the run saw.
expect frame.p50_us >= 0
expect frame.p99_us >= 0
expect frame.max_us >= 0
expect frame.gap.p99_us >= 0
expect frame.stalls 0
expect frame.p99 < 8
expect storm.rate >= 190

# D9: packet-to-pixel under 16 ms at the 99th percentile. From the packet arriving at the link
# (the storm's link stamps VehicleState::packet_in; no product link does) to the frame showing it
# presented (a callback deferred from the marker's paint, run when gpui's draw-and-present update
# ends), less the harness's work. Each packet once, at the first frame that shows it. The journey
# includes the wait for the link's next snapshot publish (5 ms) and for the next frame.
# Measured 2026-09-26, release build, quiet machine (the run holds the build lock): with the
# earlier 20 ms publish cadence p50 15.6 ms, p99 25.3 ms, max 26.2 ms, 228 of 469 over 16 ms,
# and 101 of 570 frames showing the snapshot the frame before had; with the 5 ms cadence p50
# 9.8 ms, p99 14.7 ms, max 18.4 ms, 2 of 569 over, 569 distinct snapshots in 570 frames.
# Frames both runs: p50 2.6 ms, p99 3.8-3.9 ms, max 5.4-5.6 ms, 0 stalls, storm.rate 199.
# storm.latency.p99 is whole milliseconds rounded down, so `< 16` holds exactly when it is under.
expect storm.latency.count >= 50
expect storm.latency.p50_us >= 0
expect storm.latency.p99_us >= 0
expect storm.latency.max_us >= 0
expect storm.latency.over >= 0
expect storm.latency.p99 < 16
